# NEXUS REPOSITORY – COMPLETE INSTALLATION AND JENKINS INTEGRATION

Objective: Install Nexus Repository Manager on a separate Ubuntu EC2 instance and connect it with the existing Jenkins server and build server to store Maven artifacts (JAR files).

Architecture:

GitHub → Jenkins Master → Build Server (Maven) → SonarQube → Trivy → Nexus → Docker → AWS ECR → AWS EKS

--------------------------------------------------
1. CREATE NEXUS EC2 INSTANCE
--------------------------------------------------

Go to AWS Console → EC2 → Launch Instance.

Configure:

Name: Nexus-Server
AMI: Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS
Instance Type: t3.large
Storage: 100 GB gp3
Key Pair: Existing SSH key
Security Group: Create new

Inbound Rules:

SSH – Port 22 – Your IP
Custom TCP – Port 8081 – Jenkins Security Group
Custom TCP – Port 8081 – Your IP

Launch the instance.

Note the Nexus Public IP and Private IP.

--------------------------------------------------
2. CONNECT TO NEXUS SERVER
--------------------------------------------------

ssh -i your-key.pem ubuntu@NEXUS-PUBLIC-IP

Update Ubuntu:

sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade -y

Install utilities:

sudo apt install wget curl tar -y

--------------------------------------------------
3. CREATE NEXUS LINUX USER
--------------------------------------------------

sudo useradd --system --create-home --shell /bin/bash nexus

sudo mkdir -p /opt/nexus
sudo mkdir -p /opt/sonatype-work/nexus3

sudo chown -R nexus:nexus /opt/nexus
sudo chown -R nexus:nexus /opt/sonatype-work

--------------------------------------------------
4. DOWNLOAD AND EXTRACT NEXUS
--------------------------------------------------

Open the official download page:

https://help.sonatype.com/en/download.html

Download the latest Nexus Repository Manager Community Edition Linux x86-64 TAR.GZ distribution.

Upload or download the archive to your Nexus EC2 instance.

Example, if the archive is in /tmp:

cd /tmp

sudo tar -xzf nexus-unix-x86-64-3.96.3-01.tar.gz -C /opt/nexus

Check extracted directory:

ls -l /opt/nexus

Create symbolic link using the actual extracted directory name:

sudo ln -sfn /opt/nexus/nexus-3.96.3-01 /opt/nexus/current

Set permissions:

sudo chown -R nexus:nexus /opt/nexus
sudo chown -R nexus:nexus /opt/sonatype-work

Note: Nexus versions from 3.87 include their own Java 21 runtime.

--------------------------------------------------
5. CONFIGURE NEXUS USER
--------------------------------------------------

sudo nano /opt/nexus/current/bin/nexus.rc

Add:

run_as_user="nexus"

Save and exit.

--------------------------------------------------
6. CONFIGURE NEXUS DATA DIRECTORY
--------------------------------------------------

sudo nano /opt/nexus/current/bin/nexus.vmoptions

Find the existing karaf.data setting and update it to:

-Dkaraf.data=/opt/sonatype-work/nexus3

Save and exit.

--------------------------------------------------
7. CREATE SYSTEMD SERVICE
--------------------------------------------------

sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/nexus.service

Paste:

[Unit]
Description=Nexus Repository Manager
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=forking
LimitNOFILE=65536
User=nexus
Group=nexus
ExecStart=/opt/nexus/current/bin/nexus start
ExecStop=/opt/nexus/current/bin/nexus stop
Restart=on-failure
TimeoutStartSec=600

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target

Save and exit.

Run:

sudo systemctl daemon-reload

sudo systemctl enable nexus

sudo systemctl start nexus

Check status:

sudo systemctl status nexus

Check logs:

sudo tail -f /opt/sonatype-work/nexus3/log/nexus.log

Wait until Nexus finishes starting.

--------------------------------------------------
8. ACCESS NEXUS DASHBOARD
--------------------------------------------------

Open browser:

http://NEXUS-PUBLIC-IP:8081

Get initial admin password:

sudo cat /opt/sonatype-work/nexus3/admin.password

Login:

Username: admin
Password: Initial password from the file

Set a new admin password when prompted.

--------------------------------------------------
9. CREATE MAVEN HOSTED REPOSITORY
--------------------------------------------------

Inside Nexus Dashboard:

Settings → Repositories → Create Repository → maven2 (hosted)

Configure:

Name: maven-releases
Version Policy: Release
Layout Policy: Strict
Deployment Policy: Allow redeploy

Click Create.

Repository URL:

http://NEXUS-PRIVATE-IP:8081/repository/maven-releases/

--------------------------------------------------
10. CREATE JENKINS USER IN NEXUS
--------------------------------------------------

Navigate:

Security → Users → Create local user.

Configure:

Username: jenkins-user
Password: Create a password
Status: Active

Assign the required repository read and upload permissions.

Save.

--------------------------------------------------
11. TEST CONNECTION FROM JENKINS SERVER
--------------------------------------------------

Connect to Jenkins EC2.

Run:

curl -I http://NEXUS-PRIVATE-IP:8081

Verify that Jenkins can reach Nexus.

--------------------------------------------------
12. ADD NEXUS CREDENTIALS IN JENKINS
--------------------------------------------------

Navigate:

Manage Jenkins → Credentials → System → Global Credentials → Add Credentials.

Configure:

Kind: Username with password
Username: jenkins-user
Password: Nexus user password
ID: nexus-creds

Save.

--------------------------------------------------
13. CONFIGURE MAVEN POM.XML
--------------------------------------------------

In your Java application's pom.xml, add:

<distributionManagement>
    <repository>
        <id>nexus-releases</id>
        <url>http://NEXUS-PRIVATE-IP:8081/repository/maven-releases/</url>
    </repository>
</distributionManagement>

--------------------------------------------------
14. CONFIGURE MAVEN SETTINGS.XML
--------------------------------------------------

On the Jenkins build agent, create:

mkdir -p ~/.m2

nano ~/.m2/settings.xml

Paste:

<settings>
    <servers>
        <server>
            <id>nexus-releases</id>
            <username>jenkins-user</username>
            <password>YOUR_NEXUS_PASSWORD</password>
        </server>
    </servers>
</settings>

Save the file.

The repository ID in pom.xml and server ID in settings.xml must match.

--------------------------------------------------
15. UPLOAD ARTIFACT TO NEXUS
--------------------------------------------------

From your Java project directory, execute:

mvn clean deploy

This performs:

1. Compiles Java source code.
2. Runs Maven tests.
3. Packages the application.
4. Generates the JAR artifact.
5. Uploads the JAR to Nexus.

--------------------------------------------------
16. VERIFY ARTIFACT IN NEXUS
--------------------------------------------------

Open:

Nexus Dashboard → Browse → maven-releases

Verify your uploaded JAR.

Example:

com/example/banking-app/1.0.0/banking-app-1.0.0.jar

--------------------------------------------------
17. USEFUL NEXUS COMMANDS
--------------------------------------------------

Start:

sudo systemctl start nexus

Stop:

sudo systemctl stop nexus

Restart:

sudo systemctl restart nexus

Status:

sudo systemctl status nexus

Logs:

sudo journalctl -u nexus -f

Check port:

sudo ss -tulnp | grep 8081

Check disk:

df -h

Check memory:

free -h

--------------------------------------------------
FINAL RESULT
--------------------------------------------------

Nexus Repository Manager is installed on a separate EC2 instance.

Jenkins communicates with Nexus through its private IP.

Maven builds the Java application and generates the JAR file.

Jenkins uses Maven deploy to upload the JAR file to Nexus.

Nexus stores and manages the application artifacts.

The stored artifact can subsequently be used by the Docker build stage to create a container image for AWS ECR and deployment to AWS EKS.

Official documentation:

https://help.sonatype.com/en/installation.html

https://help.sonatype.com/en/download.html
